# /etc/fw_env.config — fw_setenv/fw_printenv U-Boot env partition map
#
# Tells fw_setenv where U-Boot's environment lives. If this is absent or
# wrong, fw_setenv silently fails: the bootcount-reset watchdog stops
# working and recovery env vars never get written.
#
# Format (one line per redundant copy; whitespace separated):
#   Device name            Offset    Env size   [Flash sector size]  [Sectors]
#
# Creality Hi (Allwinner T113) layout. Verified against real hardware:
#
#   1. Stock /etc/fw_env.config, read out of three independent captures of
#      the shipping image (live rootfs backup, its /rom copy, and the
#      unpacked stock squashfs). All three carry exactly the two data lines
#      below, byte for byte.
#   2. Full eMMC dump taken 2026-07-14. Its /dev/by-name listing resolves
#      env -> /dev/mmcblk0p2 and env-redund -> /dev/mmcblk0p3, and
#      /proc/partitions gives both partitions as 1024 blocks = 1 MiB.
#
#   So the partitions are 1 MiB each and the U-Boot environment occupies the
#   first 128 KiB (0x20000) of each. The environment is not the whole
#   partition.
#
#   The names are "env" and "env-redund". There is no env0 / env1 on this
#   board, and pointing fw_setenv at those names makes every write fail.
#
# Flash sector size and sector count are omitted deliberately, exactly as
# stock does. An omitted sector size defaults to the environment size, which
# is the value we want, and sector count is meaningless for an MMC block
# device. Matching stock byte for byte beats restating defaults.
#
# Confirm on your own unit before trusting it:
#   ls -l /dev/by-name/env*      and      cat /proc/partitions

/dev/by-name/env	       0x0000          0x20000
/dev/by-name/env-redund        0x0000          0x20000
